1
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:12,920
Hello, everybody.

2
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:17,080
Welcome back to the IELTS Intermediate Speaking Course by PrEP.

3
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:22,240
In this lesson, we will focus on part one in the speaking test.

4
00:00:23,340 --> 00:00:28,280
Part one of the IELTS speaking test lasts around four to five minutes.

5
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:32,260
In this part, you'll be asked questions about yourself.

6
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:40,300
The topics can be familiar or unfamiliar, but all the questions will be about you and your experiences.

7
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:46,900
In this video, we will look at seven main types of IELTS speaking part one questions.

8
00:00:52,220 --> 00:00:55,200
The first question type is personal habits.

9
00:00:55,820 --> 00:01:00,040
These questions ask about what you usually do in your daily life.

10
00:01:00,260 --> 00:01:06,160
The topics often include your daily work and study routine, morning habits,

11
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:09,800
or more specific ones such as shopping or reading.

12
00:01:10,380 --> 00:01:16,240
These questions usually start with what do you usually do or do you often?

13
00:01:17,020 --> 00:01:23,160
To get a high score, you need to use adverbs of frequency like normally or regularly.

14
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:27,120
Don't just say what you do, explain the routine.

15
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:31,340
For example, what do you usually do in the morning?

16
00:01:32,060 --> 00:01:35,340
I will give you 30 seconds to think about your answer.

17
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:37,740
30 seconds start now.

18
00:02:10,830 --> 00:02:14,330
A good answer would be I tend to wake up quite early

19
00:02:14,330 --> 00:02:17,210
around 6 a.m. and go for a quick run.

20
00:02:17,890 --> 00:02:22,390
After that, I usually have a light breakfast before starting my studies for the day.

21
00:02:23,450 --> 00:02:27,910
The second type is preferences or in other words, you will be

22
00:02:27,910 --> 00:02:31,810
asked about likes and dislikes in a wide range of topics.

23
00:02:32,390 --> 00:02:40,550
They can include but are not limited to food, types of transport, music, TV, art, clothing and so many others.

24
00:02:41,170 --> 00:02:45,010
For this question type, you will hear phrases like do you prefer

25
00:02:45,010 --> 00:02:49,190
A or B or which do you like more or what is your favorite?

26
00:02:49,610 --> 00:02:54,890
To answer, you should answer directly about your preferences and then add

27
00:02:54,890 --> 00:02:59,990
further details to extend it, which we will learn in the next videos.

28
00:03:01,050 --> 00:03:07,130
To get a high score, you should learn different ways of expressing what you like or don't like.

29
00:03:07,690 --> 00:03:11,950
For example, do you prefer traveling by train or bus?

30
00:03:12,890 --> 00:03:15,830
You have 30 seconds starting now.

31
00:03:48,830 --> 00:03:53,220
To answer, you could say, I'd rather travel by train, it's significantly

32
00:03:53,220 --> 00:03:58,920
more comfortable than the bus and I find I can actually relax or read a book while I travel.

33
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:02,720
Type 3 is past experiences.

34
00:04:03,260 --> 00:04:07,040
While the first two types mainly require you to use the present

35
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:12,740
tenses, this is a test of your grammar, specifically your past tenses.

36
00:04:13,190 --> 00:04:17,760
You will often be asked about your past experiences in questions about

37
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:21,079
your childhood, your hometown or traveling.

38
00:04:21,740 --> 00:04:29,360
These questions have phrases like did you, when you were a child or how has something changed?

39
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:35,300
To answer this question type, you must use past tense verbs correctly

40
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:42,640
and try to extend your answer with details, for example, an event in the past or a memory.

41
00:04:43,340 --> 00:04:47,160
For example, did you enjoy sports when you were at school?

42
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:51,140
You have 30 seconds to think and answer.

43
00:05:24,610 --> 00:05:30,870
My answer is yes, I vividly remember playing football every single afternoon with my classmates.

44
00:05:31,170 --> 00:05:35,850
I was quite competitive back then and I loved the feeling of being part of a team.

45
00:05:36,710 --> 00:05:40,530
The next question type is questions about frequency.

46
00:05:41,450 --> 00:05:45,950
This is similar to questions about habits, but the focus is strictly

47
00:05:45,950 --> 00:05:53,570
on how often you do an activity like visiting family, going to the cinema or using social media.

48
00:05:54,390 --> 00:05:59,030
So the topics can vary for this type, from common topics like

49
00:05:59,030 --> 00:06:04,670
friends and family, hometown TV, to slightly less common ones like computers

50
00:06:04,670 --> 00:06:08,330
and technology, newspapers or gifts.

51
00:06:08,970 --> 00:06:14,390
These questions almost always start with how often do you, and to

52
00:06:14,390 --> 00:06:19,350
answer, you should use adverbs of frequency and varied time phrases.

53
00:06:19,990 --> 00:06:24,750
You should avoid saying always every time, but instead you can use

54
00:06:24,750 --> 00:06:32,290
phrases like on a regular basis or once in a while, for example, how often do you go to the cinema?

55
00:06:32,930 --> 00:06:36,170
Your 30 seconds to think and answer starts now.

56
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:17,060
You might answer as, to be honest, I go once in a blue moon, I find it much more convenient and cheaper to just

57
00:07:17,060 --> 00:07:19,440
watch movies at home on my laptop.

58
00:07:21,100 --> 00:07:22,720
Next we have comparisons.

59
00:07:23,140 --> 00:07:29,960
This question type requires you to compare two things, like living in the city versus the countryside.

60
00:07:30,540 --> 00:07:37,300
These questions usually contain phrases like, is it better to, or what are the differences between?

61
00:07:37,900 --> 00:07:43,180
To answer, you should use comparative adjectives and linking devices to show

62
00:07:43,180 --> 00:07:50,420
the relationships between two things, for example, is it better to study alone or with friends?

63
00:07:50,910 --> 00:07:54,700
And you have 30 seconds to answer, starting now.

64
00:08:28,320 --> 00:08:31,220
An answer could be, it depends on the subject.

65
00:08:32,140 --> 00:08:36,700
Studying alone is better for deep focus, whereas studying with friends is

66
00:08:36,700 --> 00:08:40,320
great for brainstorming or discussing difficult concepts.

67
00:08:42,100 --> 00:08:45,260
The next question type is questions about opinions.

68
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:49,820
The examiner will ask what you think about a certain topic.

69
00:08:50,200 --> 00:08:54,000
You might think that this sounds more like a question type in

70
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:59,260
IELTS Part 3, but you can still find opinion questions in Part 1.

71
00:08:59,640 --> 00:09:04,540
The big difference is that while Part 3 questions ask about more

72
00:09:04,540 --> 00:09:11,020
abstract or broader issues, Part 1 questions ask about your opinion on daily matters.

73
00:09:11,800 --> 00:09:17,800
Your answers in Part 1 are only shorter, preferably with only 2 -3 sentences.

74
00:09:18,100 --> 00:09:26,540
This question usually starts with, what do you think about, or do you think it is good or important to?

75
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:31,640
To answer, you can open with phrases such as, in my opinion,

76
00:09:32,140 --> 00:09:39,040
in my view, or as I see it, before sharing your opinion and further expansion details.

77
00:09:39,660 --> 00:09:48,000
For example, do you think it is important to learn a second language, and 30 seconds for you to think and answer begins.

78
00:10:22,100 --> 00:10:25,660
A good response is, in my opinion, it is vital.

79
00:10:26,220 --> 00:10:28,100
It doesn't just help with communication.

80
00:10:28,540 --> 00:10:32,600
It also opens your mind to different cultures and ways of thinking.

81
00:10:34,160 --> 00:10:38,740
Finally, we have questions about future predictions or likelihood, where you

82
00:10:38,740 --> 00:10:43,900
will be asked about a future plan or whether you think something will happen in the future.

83
00:10:44,320 --> 00:10:48,160
Therefore, you will need to use future tenses and the answers.

84
00:10:48,800 --> 00:10:53,100
You may find this question type in topics such as work and

85
00:10:53,100 --> 00:10:56,440
study, travel, technology, and so on.

86
00:10:56,700 --> 00:11:02,040
The questions may start with, do you think you will, or is

87
00:11:02,040 --> 00:11:06,900
it likely that, since we don't know the future for sure, when

88
00:11:06,900 --> 00:11:16,060
answering these questions you should use probability words like, I expect, possibly, or it's highly likely.

89
00:11:16,900 --> 00:11:20,880
For example, do you think you will live in another country in

90
00:11:20,880 --> 00:11:25,300
the future, and you have 30 seconds, starting now.

91
00:11:59,010 --> 00:12:01,230
You could say, I think I will.

92
00:12:02,070 --> 00:12:05,980
After graduation, I have decided to continue improving my English

93
00:12:05,980 --> 00:12:10,570
through self -study so I can eventually move to the UK for my master's degree.

94
00:12:16,210 --> 00:12:20,510
Now let's review what we learned so far through some interactive questions.

95
00:12:21,790 --> 00:12:22,670
Great job, everyone.

96
00:12:23,550 --> 00:12:27,690
Understanding these seven question types will help you answer each of them better.

97
00:12:28,270 --> 00:12:35,090
In the following videos, we will go over techniques to brainstorm ideas and extend our answers in part one.

98
00:12:35,730 --> 00:12:36,510
I'll see you soon.

